ee, 2 days @ 35.00/day.” The labor also changes from replacing front pads to replacing front pads and rotors, and from 1.5 to 2.4 hours. These differences are labeled **Question this** below because they are not shown on the signed estimate.\n\n## Labels\n| Line | Label | Reason (quoted) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Estimate 1 — “Front brake pads, ceramic” | Check | The part has a price but no OEM, aftermarket, remanufactured, or salvage indication. |\n| Estimate 2 — “Labor — replace front brake pads, book time 1.5 hr @ 145.00/hr” | Standard | The operation, hours, and rate are shown. |\n| Estimate 3 — “Shop supplies” | Check | The flat fee does not say what it includes. |\n| Estimate 4 — “Sales tax 7.25%” | Standard | A tax rate is stated. |\n| Invoice 5 — “Front brake pads, ceramic” | Check | The part has a price but no OEM, aftermarket, remanufactured, or salvage indication. |\n| Invoice 6 — “Front brake rotors, pair” | Question this | This work is not listed on the signed estimate. |\n| Invoice 7 — “Brake fluid flush” | Question this | This work is not listed on the signed estimate. |\n| Invoice 8 — “Labor — replace front pads and rotors, 2.4 hr @ 145.00/hr” | Question this | The operation and hours differ from the signed estimate’s front-pad replacement labor. |\n| Invoice 9 — “Storage fee, 2 days @ 35.00/day” | Question this | This charge is not listed on the signed estimate. |\n| Invoice 10 — “Sales tax 7.25% on parts and labor” | Standard | A tax rate and tax base are stated. |\n\n## Questions to ask\n1. Which added work was authorized, and when was that authorization given? — a good answer identifies each item, when you agreed to it, and whether it was written or verbal.\n2. Why were the rotors and brake-fluid flush added, and where is that work documented? — a good answer explains the reason for each item and points to the relevant paperwork or record.\n3. Why did the labor operation and hours change from the estimate? — a good answer explains the added work and how the billed hours relate to it.\n4. What caused the two-day storage charge, and where was that charge disclosed or authorized? — a good answer explains the dates and circumstances and identifies the applicable paperwork.\n\n## Decision\nPhotograph the estimate and invoice, note what was promised verbally and by whom, and keep the paperwork chain. The user decides; this reading does not approve or decline the charges.\n\n## Needs confirmation\n[CONFIRM: What the shop-supplies fee covers.]  \n[CONFIRM: Whether and when the added work and storage charge were authorized.]  \n[NOT SHOWN: The parts tier for the brake pads and rotors.]","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Evaluating invoice discrepancies**\n\nI'm noticing some mismatches in the standard labor operations, hours, and rates versus the scope expansion. I should question this inconsistency. There's also an unauthorized addition in storage that I need to address. Tax standards seem to apply here, so I’ll check if the invoice aligns with those standards, especially considering potential tax implications. It’s important to ensure all parts are labeled correctly to avoid any further documentation issues.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Evaluating invoice discrepancies**\n\nI'm noticing some mismatches in the standard labor operations, hours, and rates versus the scope expansion. I should question this inconsistency. There's also an unauthorized addition in storage that I need to address. Tax standards seem to apply here, so I’ll check if the invoice aligns with those standards, especially considering potential tax implications. It’s important to ensure all parts are labeled correctly to avoid any further documentation issues.","format":"openai-responses-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"gAAAAABrA2yTQfE8jbgN8UcppIxZTpeS9vJ809mceb0YYo6iWdsvmcgo8eFSlao2UX1L3O9JwaGWlaPZnOHmccH3tYRjbh5rmxhJW9TJZU6kGoQdoByfv5eVWeDwJmuzFP6rxa-qETOpcsOx7IixEcnptpk-N2wEPRG6eYT5hP2Jn6GX7JlfFEN1mgM